Suppose that the firm is initially at a point where it is producing its long-run profit-maximizing output level of good  $L$  given prices  $w$  and  $p$ ,  $q(w, p)$  [i.e., the level that is optimal under the long-run cost conditions described by  $c(w, q)$ ], and that all inputs are optimally adjusted [i.e.,  $z_r = z_r(w, q(w, p))$  for all  $r = 1, \dots, L - 1$ , where  $z_r(\cdot, \cdot)$  is the long-run input demand function]. Show that the firm's profit-maximizing output response to a marginal increase in the price of good  $L$  is larger in the long run than in the short run. [*Hint:* Define a short-run cost function  $c_s(w, q|z_1)$  that gives the minimized costs of producing output level  $q$  given that input 1 is fixed at level  $z_1$ .]

