supply correspondence for an economy in which there is a single firm (so  $J = 1$ ).<sup>13</sup> This firm's cost function  $c(\cdot)$  is continuous and differentiable but not convex. In the figure, the light curve is the graph of the firm's marginal cost function  $c'(\cdot)$ . As the figure illustrates,  $c'(\cdot)$  fails to be nondecreasing. The heavier curve is the firm's actual supply correspondence  $q(\cdot)$  (you should verify that it is determined as indicated in the figure).<sup>14</sup> The graph of the supply correspondence no longer coincides with the marginal cost curve and, as is evident in the figure, no intersection exists between the graph of the supply correspondence and the demand curve. Thus, in this case, no competitive equilibrium exists.

