Perhaps not surprisingly, to generate the existence of an equilibrium with a determinate number of firms, the long-run cost function must exhibit a strictly positive efficient scale; that is, *there must exist a strictly positive output level  $\bar{q}$  at which a firm's average costs of production are minimized* (see Section 5.D for a further discussion of the efficient scale concept).

Suppose, in particular, that  $c'(\cdot)$  has a unique efficient scale  $\bar{q} > 0$ , and let the minimized level of average cost be  $\bar{c} = c(\bar{q})/\bar{q}$ . Assume, moreover, that  $x(\bar{c}) > 0$ . If at a long-run equilibrium  $(p^*, q^*, J^*)$  we had  $p^* > \bar{c}$ , then  $p^*\bar{q} > \bar{c}\bar{q}$ , and so we would have  $\pi(p^*) > 0$ . Thus, at any long-run equilibrium we must have  $p^* \leq \bar{c}$ . In contrast, if  $p^* < \bar{c}$ , then  $x(p^*) > 0$ ; but since  $p^*q - c(q) = p^*q - (c(q)/q)q \leq (p^* - \bar{c})q < 0$ 

