**Proof:** Take any  $y \in X$  and suppose that  $x_h > y$  (the argument is the same for  $y > x_h$ ). We need to show that  $y$  does not defeat  $x$ , that is, that

$$\#\{i \in I : x_h > i y\} \geq \#\{i \in I : y > i x_h\}.$$

Consider the set of agents  $S \subset I$  that have peaks *larger than or equal* to  $x_h$ , that is,  $S = \{i \in I : x_i \geq x_h\}$ . Then  $x_i \geq x_h > y$  for every  $i \in S$ . Hence, by single-peakedness of  $\gtrsim_i$  with respect to  $\geq$ , we get  $x_h > i y$  for every  $i \in S$ . On the other hand, because agent  $h$  is a median agent we have that  $\#S \geq I/2$  and so  $\#\{i \in I : y > i x_h\} \leq \#(I \setminus S) \leq I/2 \leq \#S \leq \#\{i \in I : x_h > i y\}$ . ■

Proposition 21.D.1 guarantees that the preference relation  $\hat{F}(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I)$  is acyclic. It may, however, not be transitive. In Exercise 21.D.6 you are asked to find an example of nontransitivity. Transitivity obtains in the special case where  $I$  is odd and, for every  $i$ , the preference relation  $\gtrsim_i$  belongs to the class  $\mathcal{P}_{\gtrsim}^I \subset \mathcal{R}_{\gtrsim}^I$  formed by the rational preference relations  $\gtrsim$  that are single peaked with respect to  $\geq$  and have the property that no two distinct alternatives are indifferent for  $\gtrsim$ . Note that, if  $I$  is odd and preferences are in this class, then, for any pair of alternatives, there is always a strict majority for one of them against the other. Hence, in this case, a Condorcet winner necessarily defeats any other alternative.

**Proposition 21.D.2:** Suppose that  $I$  is odd and that  $\geq$  is a linear order on  $X$ . Then pairwise majority voting generates a well-defined social welfare functional  $F: \mathcal{P}_{\gtrsim}^I \rightarrow \mathcal{R}$ . That is, on the domain of preferences that are single-peaked with respect to  $\geq$  and, moreover, have the property that no two distinct alternatives are indifferent, we can conclude that the social relation  $\hat{F}(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I)$  generated by pairwise majority voting is complete and transitive.

**Proof:** We already know that  $\hat{F}(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I)$  is complete. It remains to show that it is transitive. For this purpose, suppose that  $x \hat{F}(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I) y$  and  $y \hat{F}(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I) z$ . Under our assumptions (recall that  $I$  is odd and that no individual indifference is allowed) this means that  $x$  defeats  $y$  and  $y$  defeats  $z$ . Consider the set  $X' = \{x, y, z\}$ . If preferences are restricted to this set then, relative to  $X'$ , preferences still belong to the class  $\mathcal{P}_{\gtrsim}^I$ , and therefore there is an alternative in  $X'$  that is not defeated by any

