Right diagram: Illustrates a half-space $A(y, z)$ in $X = \mathbb{R}^2$ separated by a dashed boundary line perpendicular to the line segment connecting points $y$ and $z$. A point $x$ lies inside the shaded region $A(y, z)$, which represents the set of alternatives preferred to both $y$ and $z$ or forming a geometric relationship between the points $x$, $y$, and $z$ relative to the dividing hyperplane.

example is that the cone spanned by the nonnegative combinations of the gradient vectors of the three utility functions equals the entire  $\mathbb{R}^2$  (see Figure 21.D.4). Exercises 21.D.7 and 21.D.8 provide further elaboration on this issue.

The reason why in two (or more) dimensions, quasiconcavity does not particularly help is that, in contrast with the one-dimensional case, there is no sensible way to assign a “median” to a set of points in the plane. This will become clear in the next, classical, Example 21.D.6 which we now describe.

**Example 21.D.6: Euclidean Preferences.** Suppose that the set of alternatives is  $\mathbb{R}^n$ . Agents have preferences  $\gtrsim$  represented by utility functions of the form  $u(y) = -\|y - x\|$ , where  $x$  is a fixed alternative in  $\mathbb{R}^n$ . In words:  $x$  is the most preferred alternative for  $\gtrsim$  and other alternatives are evaluated by how close they are to  $x$  in the Euclidean distance. The indifference curves of a typical consumer in  $\mathbb{R}^2$  are pictured in Figure 21.D.5.

In the current example, the set  $\mathbb{R}^n$  does double duty. On the one hand, it represents the set of alternatives. On the other, it also stands for the set of all possible preferences because every  $x \in \mathbb{R}^n$  uniquely identifies the preferences that have  $x$  as a peak.<sup>11</sup>

Given two distinct alternatives  $y, z \in \mathbb{R}^n$ , an agent will prefer  $y$  to  $z$  if and only if his peak is closer to  $y$  than to  $z$ . Thus, the region of peaks associated with preferences that prefer  $y$  to  $z$  is

$$A(y, z) = \{x \in \mathbb{R}^n: \|x - y\| < \|x - z\|\}.$$

See Figure 21.D.6 for a representation. Geometrically, the boundary of  $A(y, z)$  is the hyperplane perpendicular to the segment connecting  $y$  and  $z$  and passing through its midpoint.

We will consider the idealized limit situation where there is a continuum of agents with Euclidean preferences and the population is described by a density function  $g(x)$  defined on  $\mathbb{R}^n$ , the set of possible peaks. Then given two distinct alternatives  $y, z \in \mathbb{R}^n$ , the fraction of the total population that prefers  $y$  to  $z$ , denoted  $m_g(y, z)$ , is simply the integral of  $g(\cdot)$  over the region  $A(y, z) \subset \mathbb{R}^n$ .

When will there exist a Condorcet winner? Suppose there is an  $x^* \in \mathbb{R}^n$  with the property that *any* hyperplane through  $x^*$  divides  $\mathbb{R}^n$  into two half-spaces each having a total mass of  $\frac{1}{2}$  according to the density  $g(\cdot)$ . This point could be called a *median* for the density  $g(\cdot)$ ; it coincides with the usual concept of a median in the case  $n = 1$ . A median in this sense is a Condorcet winner. It cannot be defeated by any other alternative because if  $y \neq x^*$  then  $A(x^*, y)$  is larger than a half-space through  $x^*$  and, therefore,  $m_g(x^*, y) \geq \frac{1}{2}$ . Conversely, if  $x^*$ 

**Figure 21.D.5 (left)**  
Euclidean preferences in  $\mathbb{R}^2$ .

**Figure 21.D.6 (right)**  
The region of Euclidean preferences that prefer  $y$  to  $z$ .

11. For an example in the same spirit where the two roles are kept separate, see Grandmont (1978) and Exercise 21.D.9.

<!-- page 811 -->

![](pg_771__page_41_Figure_14.jpeg)

> **Image description:** A geometric diagram in two-dimensional space X = R^2 illustrating why a point x* that is not a median is not a Condorcet winner. The diagram shows axes with a dashed hyperplane boundary line separating a lower region from a half-space H. Parallel above the dashed line is a solid line passing through point x*. A vector arrow q is shown orthogonal to the lines, pointing into a dark cross-hatched half-space containing the point x* + \varepsilon q, representing the set A(x* + \varepsilon q, x*). A shaded strip lies between the solid line and the dashed hyperplane H. At the bottom, a formula states: \int_H g(z) dz > 1/2 \Rightarrow m_{g(\cdot)}(x* + \varepsilon q, x*) > 1/2.

**Figure 21.D.7**  
If  $x^*$  is not a median then it is not a Condorcet winner.

![](pg_771__page_41_Figure_16.jpeg)

> **Image description:** The image contains two sub-figures illustrating medians in two-dimensional spaces:

(a) Uniform distribution over a rectangle: A rectangle with a central point labeled x*. A dashed line passing through x* at an angle divides the rectangle into two equal areas, showing that the central point x* is a median as every line passing through it bisects the total area.

(b) Uniform distribution over a triangle: An equilateral triangle with a horizontal dashed line passing through a central point labeled x. The line divides the triangle into a smaller top triangular section and a larger bottom trapezoidal section, demonstrating that no point exists where every line bisects the area into equal halves, and thus there is no median.

**Figure 21.D.8**  
(a) Uniform distribution over a rectangle: The center point  $x^*$  is a median since every plane through  $x^*$  divides the rectangle into two figures of equal area.  
(b) Uniform distribution over a triangle: There is no median.

is not a median then there is a direction  $q \in \mathbb{R}^n$  such that the mass of the half-space  $\{z \in \mathbb{R}^n: q \cdot z > q \cdot x^*\}$  is larger than  $\frac{1}{2}$ . Thus, by continuity, if  $e > 0$  is small then the mass of the translated half-space  $A(x^* + eq, x^*)$  is larger than  $\frac{1}{2}$ . Hence  $x^* + eq$  defeats  $x^*$ , and  $x^*$  cannot be a Condorcet winner. (See Figure 21.D.7.)

We have seen that a Condorcet winner exists if and only if there is a median for the density  $g(\cdot)$ . But for  $n > 1$  the existence of a median imposes so many conditions (there are many half-spaces) that it becomes a knife-edge property. Figure 21.D.8 provides examples. In Figure 21.D.8(a), the density  $g(\cdot)$  is the uniform density over a rectangle [a case first studied by Tullock (1967)]. The center of the rectangle is then, indeed, a median. But the rectangle is very special. The typical case is one of nonexistence. In Figure 21.D.8(b), the density  $g(\cdot)$  is the uniform density over a triangle. Then no median exists: Through any point of a triangle we can draw a line that divides it into two regions of unequal area.<sup>12</sup> ■

12. See Caplin and Nalebuff (1988) for further analysis. They show that under a restriction on the density function (called “logarithmic concavity” and satisfied, in particular, for uniform densities over convex sets), there are always points (“generalized medians”) in  $\mathbb{R}^n$  with the property that any hyperplane through the point divides  $\mathbb{R}^n$  into two regions, each of which has mass larger than  $1/e$ . This means that these points cannot be defeated by any other alternative if the majority required is not  $\frac{1}{2}$  but any number larger than  $1 - (1/e) > \frac{1}{2}$ , 64% say. Of course, a 64% rule becomes less decisive than a 50% rule: There will now be many pairs of alternatives with the property that no member of the pair defeats the other.

<!-- page 812 -->

## 21.E Social Choice Functions

The task we set ourselves to accomplish in the previous sections was how to aggregate profiles of individual preference relations into a coherent (i.e. rational) social preference order. Presumably, this social preference order is then used to make decisions. In this section we focus directly on social decisions and pose the aggregation question as one of analyzing how profiles of individual preferences turn into social decisions.

The main result we obtain again yields a dictatorship conclusion. The result amounts, in a sense, to a translation of the Arrow's impossibility theorem into the language of choice functions. It also offers a reinterpretation of the condition of pairwise independence, and provides a link towards the incentive-based analysis of Chapter 23.

As before, we have a set of alternatives  $X$  and a finite set of agents  $I$ . The set of preference relations  $\gtrsim$  on  $X$  is denoted  $\mathcal{A}$ . We also designate by  $\mathcal{P}$  the subset of  $\mathcal{A}$  consisting of the preference relations  $\gtrsim \in \mathcal{A}$  with the property that no two distinct alternatives are indifferent for  $\gtrsim$ .

**Definition 21.E.1:** Given any subset  $\mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{A}^I$ , a *social choice function*  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$  defined on  $\mathcal{A}$  assigns a chosen element  $f(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I) \in X$  to every profile of individual preferences in  $\mathcal{A}$ .

The notion of social choice function embodies the requirement that the chosen set be single valued. We could argue that this is, after all, in the nature of what a choice is.<sup>13</sup> More restrictive is the fact that we do not allow for random choice.<sup>14</sup>

If  $X$  is finite, every social welfare functional  $F(\cdot)$  on a domain  $\mathcal{A}$  induces a natural social choice function by associating with each  $(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  a most preferred element in  $X$  for the social preference relation  $F(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I)$ . For example, if, as in Proposition 21.D.2,  $\mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{P}_{\geq}^I$  is a domain of single-peaked preferences,  $I$  is odd, and  $F(\cdot)$  is the pairwise majority voting social welfare functional defined on  $\mathcal{A}$ , then for every  $(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I)$  the choice  $f(\gtrsim_1, \dots, \gtrsim_I)$  is the Condorcet winner in  $X$ .

We now state and prove a result parallel to Arrow's impossibility theorem. Recall that for Arrow's theorem we had two conditions: the social welfare functional had to be Paretian and had to be pairwise independent. Here we require again two conditions: first, the social choice function must be, again, (*weakly*) *Paretian*; and, second, it should be *monotonic*. We define these concepts in Definitions 21.E.2 and 21.E.4, respectively.

13. Nevertheless, allowing for multivalued choice sets (that is, allowing there to be more than one acceptable social choice) is natural in some contexts, and certain assumptions on social choice may be more plausible in the multivalued case.

14. Note also the contrast between the definition of choice function here and the similar concept of choice rule in Section 1.C. There we contemplated the possibility of there being several budgets and of the choice depending on the budget at hand. Here the budget is fixed (it is always  $X$ ) but the choice may depend on the profile of underlying individual preferences. Clearly we could, but will not, consider situations that encompass both cases. Another contrast with Section 1.C is that here we limit ourselves to single-valued choice.

<!-- page 813 -->

**Definition 21.E.2:** The social choice function  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$  defined on  $\mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{R}^I$  is *weakly Paretian* if for any profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  the choice  $f(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in X$  is a weak Pareto optimum. That is, if for some pair  $\{x, y\} \subset X$  we have that  $x >_i y$  for every  $i$ , then  $y \neq f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ .

In order to define monotonicity we need a preliminary concept.

**Definition 21.E.3:** The alternative  $x \in X$  *maintains its position from* the profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}^I$  to the profile  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in \mathcal{R}^I$  if

$$x z_i y \text{ implies } x z'_i y$$

for every  $i$  and every  $y \in X$ .

In other words,  $x$  maintains its position from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  to  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  if for every  $i$  the set of alternatives inferior (or indifferent) to  $x$  expands (or remains the same) in moving from  $z_i$  to  $z'_i$ . That is,

$$L(x, z_i) = \{y \in X : x z_i y\} \subset L(x, z'_i) = \{y \in X : x z'_i y\}.$$

Note that the condition stated in Definition 21.E.3 imposes no restriction on how other alternatives different from  $x$  may change their mutual order in going from  $z_i$  to  $z'_i$ .<sup>15</sup>

**Definition 21.E.4:** The social choice function  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$  defined on  $\mathcal{A} \subset \mathcal{R}^I$  is *monotonic* if for any two profiles  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$ ,  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  with the property that the chosen alternative  $x = f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  maintains its position from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  to  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$ , we have that  $f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) = x$ .

In words: The social choice function is monotonic if no alternative can be dropped from being chosen unless for some agent its disirability deteriorates.

Are there social choice functions that are weakly Paretian and monotonic? The answer is “yes.” For example, in Exercise 21.E.1 you are asked to verify that the pairwise majority voting social decision function defined on a domain of single-peaked preferences is weakly Paretian and monotonic. But what if we have a universal domain (i.e.,  $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{R}^I$  or  $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{P}^I$ )? A not very attractive class of social choice functions having the two properties in this domain are the *dictatorial* social choice functions.

**Definition 21.E.5:** An agent  $h \in I$  is a *dictator* for the social choice function  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$  if, for every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$ ,  $f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  is a most preferred alternative for  $z_h$  in  $X$ ; that is,

$$f(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \{x \in X : x z_h y \text{ for every } y \in X\}.$$

A social choice function that admits a dictator is called *dictatorial*.

In the domain  $\mathcal{P}^I$ , a dictatorial social choice function is weakly Paretian and monotonic. (This is clear enough, but at any rate you should verify it in Exercise 21.E.2, where you are also asked to discuss the case  $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{R}^I$ .) Unfortunately, in the universal domain we cannot get anything better than the dictatorial social choice functions. The impossibility result of Proposition 21.E.1 establishes this.

15. As in Section 3.B, the sets  $L(x, z_i)$  are also referred to as *lower contour sets*.

<!-- page 814 -->

**Proposition 21.E.1:** Suppose that the number of alternatives is at least three and that the domain of admissible preference profiles is either  $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{R}^1$  or  $\mathcal{A} = \mathcal{P}^1$ . Then every weakly Paretian and monotonic social choice function  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$  is dictatorial.

**Proof:** The proof of the result will be obtained as a corollary of Arrow's impossibility theorem (Proposition 21.C.1). To this effect, we proceed to derive a social welfare functional  $F(\cdot)$  that rationalizes  $f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  for every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$ . We will then show that  $F(\cdot)$  satisfies the assumptions of Arrow's theorem, hence yielding the dictatorship conclusion.

We begin with a useful definition.

**Definition 21.E.6:** Given a finite subset  $X' \subset X$  and a profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{R}^1$ , we say that the profile  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  takes  $X'$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  if, for every  $i$ ,

$$\begin{aligned} x >'_i y && \text{for } x \in X' \text{ and } y \notin X', \\ x &z'_i y \Leftrightarrow x z'_i y && \text{for all } x, y \in X'. \end{aligned}$$

In words: The preference relation  $z'_i$  is obtained from  $z_i$  by simply taking every alternative in  $X'$  to the top, while preserving the internal (weak or strict) ordering among these alternatives. The ordering among alternatives not in  $X'$  is arbitrary. For example, if  $x >'_i y >'_i z >'_i w$ , then the preference relation  $>'_i$  defined by  $y >'_i w >'_i z >'_i x$  takes  $\{y, w\}$  to the top from  $z_i$ . Note also that if  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  takes  $X'$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ , then every  $x \in X'$  maintains its position in going from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  to  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$ .

For the rest of the proof we proceed in steps:

*Step 1:* If both the profiles  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  and  $(z''_1, \dots, z''_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  take  $X' \subset X$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ , then  $f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) = f(z''_1, \dots, z''_I)$ .

For every  $i$  and  $x \in X'$  we have

$$\{y \in X: x z'_i y\} = \{y \in X: x z''_i y\} = \{y \in X: x z_i y\} \cup X \setminus X'.$$

By the weak Pareto property,  $f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in X'$ . Thus,  $f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in X'$  maintains its position in going from  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  to  $(z''_1, \dots, z''_I)$ . Therefore, by the monotonicity of  $f(\cdot)$ , we conclude that  $f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) = f(z''_1, \dots, z''_I)$ .

*Step 2: Definition of  $F(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ .*

For every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  we define a certain binary relation  $F(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  on  $X$ . Specifically, we let  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$ , (read as " $x$  is socially at least as good as  $y$ ") if  $x = y$  or if  $x = f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  when  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  is any profile that takes  $\{x, y\} \subset X$  to the top from the profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ . By step 1 this is well defined, that is, independent of the particular profile  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  chosen.

*Step 3: For every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$ ,  $F(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  is a rational preference relation. Moreover,  $F(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{P}$ ; that is, no two distinct alternatives are socially indifferent.*

Because  $f(\cdot)$  is weakly Paretian, it follows that when  $(z'_1, \dots, z'_I)$  takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  we must have  $f(z'_1, \dots, z'_I) \in \{x, y\}$ . Therefore, we conclude that either  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$  or  $y F(z_1, \dots, z_I) x$ , but, because of step 1, not both (unless  $x = y$ ). In particular,  $F(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  is complete.

<!-- page 815 -->

To verify transitivity, suppose that  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$  and  $y F(z_1, \dots, z_I) z$ . We can assume that the three alternatives  $\{x, y, z\}$  are distinct. Let  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'') \in \mathcal{A}$  be a profile that takes  $\{x, y, z\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ . Because  $f(\cdot)$  is weakly Paretian, we have  $f(z_1'', \dots, z_I'') \in \{x, y, z\}$ .

Suppose that we had  $y = f(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ . Consider a profile  $(z_1', \dots, z_I') \in \mathcal{A}$  that takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ . Since  $y$  maintains its position from  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$  to  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ , it follows from monotonicity that  $f(z_1', \dots, z_I') = y$ . But  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$  also takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ : the relative ordering of  $x$  and  $y$ , the two alternatives at the top, has not been altered in any individual preference in going from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  to  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ . Therefore we conclude that  $y F(z_1, \dots, z_I) x$ , which contradicts the assumption that  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$ ,  $x \neq y$ . Hence,  $y \neq f(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ .

Similarly, we obtain  $z \neq f(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ . We only need to repeat the same argument using the pair  $\{y, z\}$  (you are asked to do so in Exercise 21.E.3).

The only possibility left is  $x = f(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ . Thus, let  $(z_1', \dots, z_I') \in \mathcal{A}$  take  $\{x, z\}$  to the top from  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ . Since  $x$  maintains its position in going from  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$  to  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ , it follows that  $x = f(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ . But  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$  also takes  $\{x, z\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ . Thus,  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) z$ , and transitivity is established.

*Step 4: The social welfare functional  $F: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}$  rationalizes  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$ ; that is, for every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$ ,  $f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  is a most preferred alternative for  $F(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  in  $X$ .*

This is intuitive enough since  $F(\cdot)$  has been constructed from  $f(\cdot)$ . Denote  $x = f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  and let  $y \neq x$  be any other alternative. Consider a profile  $(z_1', \dots, z_I') \in \mathcal{A}$  that takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ . Since  $x$  maintains position from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  to  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ , we have  $x = f(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ . Therefore,  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$ .

*Step 5: The social welfare functional  $F: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}$  is Paretian.*

Clear if  $x >_i y$  for every  $i$  then, by the Paretian property of  $f(\cdot)$ , we must have  $x = f(z_1', \dots, z_I')$  whenever  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$  takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$ . Hence  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$ , and by step 3 we conclude that  $x F_p(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$ .

*Step 6: The social welfare functional  $F: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow \mathcal{P}$  satisfies the pairwise independence condition.*

This follows from step 1. Suppose that  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  and  $(z_1', \dots, z_I') \in \mathcal{A}$  have the same ordering of  $\{x, y\}$  for every  $i$  (that is, for every  $i$ ,  $x z_i y$  if and only if  $x z_i' y$ ). Suppose that  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'') \in \mathcal{A}$  takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  and that, say,  $x = f(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$ . Then  $x F(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$ . But  $(z_1'', \dots, z_I'')$  also takes  $\{x, y\}$  to the top from  $(z_1', \dots, z_I')$ . Hence,  $x F(z_1', \dots, z_I') y$ , as we wanted to prove.

*Step 7: The social choice function  $f: \mathcal{A} \rightarrow X$  is dictatorial.*

By Arrow's theorem (Proposition 21.C.1) there is an agent  $h \in I$  such that for every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_I) \in \mathcal{A}$  we have  $x F_p(z_1, \dots, z_I) y$  whenever  $x >_h y$ . Therefore,  $f(z_1, \dots, z_I)$  [which by step 4 is a most preferred alternative for

<!-- page 816 -->

 $F(z_1, \dots, z_l)$  in  $X$ ] must also be a most preferred alternative for  $h$ ; that is,  $f(z_1, \dots, z_l) z_h x$  for every  $x \in X$ . Hence agent  $h$  is a dictator. ■

Finally, we mention the following corollary (Proposition 21.E.2) to hint at the connection between Proposition 21.E.1 and the issue of incentives to truthful preference revelation, a topic that is studied extensively in Chapter 23.

**Proposition 21.E.2:** Suppose that the number of alternatives is at least three and that  $f: \mathcal{P}^l \rightarrow X$  is a social choice function that is weakly Paretian and satisfies the following *no-incentive-to-misrepresent* condition:

 $f(z_1, \dots, z_{h-1}, z_h, z_{h+1}, \dots, z_l) z_h f(z_1, \dots, z_{h-1}, z'_h, z_{h+1}, \dots, z_l)$  for every agent  $h$ , every  $z'_h \in \mathcal{P}$ , and every profile  $(z_1, \dots, z_l) \in \mathcal{P}^l$ . Then  $f(\cdot)$  is dictatorial.

**Proof:** In view of Proposition 21.E.1 it suffices to show that  $f: \mathcal{P}^l \rightarrow X$  must be monotonic.

Suppose that it is not. Then without loss of generality we can assume that, for some agent  $h$ , there are preferences  $z_i \in \mathcal{P}$  for agents  $i \neq h$ , and preferences  $z_h''$ ,  $z_h''' \in \mathcal{P}$  for agent  $h$ , such that, denoting

$$x = f(z_1, \dots, z_{h-1}, z_h'', z_{h+1}, \dots, z_l)$$

and

$$y = f(z_1, \dots, z_{h-1}, z_h''', z_{h+1}, \dots, z_l),$$

we have that  $x z_h'' z$  implies  $x z_h''' z$  for every  $z \in X$ , and yet  $y \neq x$ .

There are two possibilities: Either  $y >_h'' x$  or  $x z_h'' y$ .

If  $y >_h'' x$  then the no-incentive-to-misrepresent condition is violated for the “true” preference relation  $z_h = z_h''$  and the misrepresentation  $z_h' = z_h''$ .

If  $x z_h'' y$  then  $x z_h''' y$ . Therefore, since no two distinct alternatives can be indifferent,  $x >_h'' y$ . But if  $x >_h''' y$  then the no-incentive-to-misrepresent condition is violated for the “true” preference relation  $z_h = z_h''$  and the misrepresentation  $z_h' = z_h''$ . ■

